{
  "result": "There is a particular kind of silence that belongs to libraries just before closing. It is not the absence of sound, but a gathering of small ones: a chair sliding back, a page turning, rain tapping against a window. In that silence, every person seems to be finishing a different journey.\n\nAt one table, a student closes a chemistry book with the solemn satisfaction of someone who has survived a difficult conversation. Near the shelves, a child bargains for one more story. An older man returns a novel he has read twice and borrows another by the same author. The librarian stamps a due date, and for a moment the future becomes a small square of ink.\n\nI like to imagine that books remember these evenings. They remember the warmth of hands, the crumbs hidden in their bindings, the pauses where a sentence made someone look up. They do not mind being forgotten for a while. They have learned patience from trees.\n\nOutside, the rain eases. The student pulls on a coat. The child leaves carrying three stories instead of one. The older man steps carefully around a puddle that holds an upside-down streetlight. Soon the librarian switches off the lamps, and the windows become mirrors.\n\nTomorrow the doors will open again. Someone will ask a question they are embarrassed to ask. Someone else will find an answer they were not looking for. The books will wait on their shelves, ordinary, until a pair of hands brings them to life.",
